{"entries":[{"timestamp":1791996555614,"editorVersion":"9.1.1","changes":[{"type":"edited","filename":"pxt.json","patch":[{"start1":450,"length1":44,"diffs":[[1,""]]}]},{"type":"added","filename":"test.ts","value":"// tests go here; this will not be compiled when this package is used as an extension.\n"},{"type":"added","filename":"main.blocks","value":"<xml xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">\n  <block type=\"pxt-on-start\"></block>\n  <block type=\"device_forever\"></block>\n</xml>"},{"type":"added","filename":"main.ts","value":"\n"}]}],"snapshots":[{"timestamp":1791996555613,"editorVersion":"9.1.1","text":{"README.md":"# ENGG 1102\n\n[Getting started with UNB IDE](projects/getting-started.md) is a small simulator-first exercise: show a greeting, respond to logical button A with a heart, and clear the micro:bit display with logical button B. It does not use BLiXels or declare course syllabus or assessment requirements.\n\n- Publication reviewer: the UNB-ECE repository maintainer. Course/instructional approval from the course instructor is pending; retain the **Instructor preview** card label.\n- Uchechi's workflow: author lessons and assets in their own repository, preview/debug with the UNB IDE Tutorial Tool, then propose a synchronization PR into this collection. The source repository and synchronization action still need configuration under [#182](https://github.com/UNB-ECE/unb-platform/issues/182).\n- Check registered dependencies, image paths, source revision and proposed catalog metadata during import. Never replace other collections or central configuration wholesale.\n- Any physical test needs separate board, firmware, program and outcome evidence. This introduction is runnable in the simulator and makes no physical-validation claim.\n\n","catalog.md":"# ENGG 1102\n\nIntroductory editor practice. Course-specific learning objectives and assessment require instructor review.\n\n## Activities\n\n```codecard\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Getting started with UNB IDE\",\n    \"description\": \"Display a greeting, show a heart with button A, and clear the micro:bit display with button B. Start in the simulator.\",\n    \"url\": \"/unb-tutorials/engg1102/projects/getting-started\",\n    \"cardType\": \"tutorial\",\n    \"imageUrl\": \"/unb-tutorials/engg1102/static/getting-started.svg\",\n    \"label\": \"Instructor preview\",\n    \"labelClass\": \"orange ribbon\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"micro led tutorial\",\n    \"description\": \"Display a radom points in the displayboard.\",\n    \"url\": \"/unb-tutorials/engg1102/projects/micro-led\",\n    \"cardType\": \"tutorial\",\n    \"imageUrl\": \"/unb-tutorials/engg1102/static/getting-started.svg\",\n    \"label\": \"Instructor preview\",\n    \"labelClass\": \"orange ribbon\"\n  }\n]\n```\n","projects/README.md":"\n> Open this page at [https://unb-ece.github.io/unb-tutorials/](https://unb-ece.github.io/unb-tutorials/)\n# engg1102 lessons\n  now it is from the unb-tutorials-fork repo\n## Use as Extension\n\nThis repository can be added as an **extension** in MakeCode.\n\n* open [/](/)\n* click on **New Project**\n* click on **Extensions** under the gearwheel menu\n* search for **https://github.com/unb-ece/unb-tutorials/engg1102/projects** and import\n\n## Edit this project\n\nTo edit this repository in MakeCode.\n\n* open [/](/)\n* click on **Import** then click on **Import URL**\n* paste **https://github.com/unb-ece/unb-tutorials/engg1102/projects** and click import\n\n#### Metadata (used for search, rendering)\n\n* for PXT/microbit\n<script src=\"https://makecode.com/gh-pages-embed.js\"></script><script>makeCodeRender(\"{{ site.makecode.home_url }}\", \"{{ site.github.owner_name }}/{{ site.github.repository_name }}\");</script>\n","projects/getting-started.md":"# ENGG 1102: getting started with UNB IDE\n\n\n## Welcome @showdialog\n\nPractice building and testing a small micro:bit program in UNB IDE. This is an introductory editor exercise, not a statement of ENGG 1102 syllabus or assessment requirements. You can complete it in the simulator. For an optional download, ask your instructor for a micro:bit or an approved UNBdev.board, a data-capable USB cable and the correct power setup.\n\nThe micro:bit display is the 5 × 5 red LED grid. It is separate from the five BLiXel colour lights on UNBdev.board. This lesson does not control the BLiXels, motors or Click boards. Keep conductive objects away from powered hardware and disconnect it if it becomes hot.\n\n## Display a greeting\n\nIn **on start**, add **show string** from **Basic** and enter **HELLO**. Select the simulator's restart button if it has stopped. Watch the greeting scroll across the red display. The greeting runs once when the program starts.\n\n```blocks\nbasic.showString(\"HELLO\")\n```\n\n## Add a button response\n\nAdd **on button A pressed** from **Input**. Inside it, add **show icon** from **Basic** and choose the heart. Leave your greeting in **on start**. Click and release A in the simulator; a heart should appear after any greeting has finished.\n\n```blocks\nbasic.showString(\"HELLO\")\ninput.onButtonPressed(Button.A, function () {\n    basic.showIcon(IconNames.Heart)\n})\n```\n\n## Add a clear button\n\nAdd **on button B pressed** and put **clear screen** from **Basic** inside it. Click and release A to show the heart, then B to clear the red display. This clear screen block affects the micro:bit display; it is not the UNBdev.board BLiXel clear command.\n\n```blocks\nbasic.showString(\"HELLO\")\ninput.onButtonPressed(Button.A, function () {\n    basic.showIcon(IconNames.Heart)\n})\ninput.onButtonPressed(Button.B, function () {\n    basic.clearScreen()\n})\n```\n\n## Check and save your work\n\nRestart the simulator: HELLO should scroll. After the greeting finishes, briefly press and release A to show the heart, then B to clear the display. If the result differs, compare your event handlers with the previous step and check that their blocks are inside the correct event. Name and save your project using the editor's project controls before closing it.\n\nIf your instructor asks you to download, use the editor's Download flow and the approved cable/setup. On some older UNBdev.boards, the printed A/B labels are reversed relative to logical A/B; ask your instructor to identify the buttons. Simulator success verifies this exercise's program flow, not physical UNBdev.board compatibility. Record any physical test separately with the board, firmware and program revisions.\n\n## Explain what happened\n\nTell a partner which code runs on start and which code waits for a button event. Change HELLO to your own short greeting, restart and repeat the A/B check. Ask your instructor for the next course activity; no additional board hardware is needed for this exercise.\n","projects/main.blocks":"<xml xmlns=\"https://developers.google.com/blockly/xml\"><variables></variables><block type=\"device_forever\" id=\"N%s{J]^L6N4/uQd1%qw4\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\"><statement name=\"HANDLER\"><block type=\"device_led_toggle\" id=\"#Yczf}/48j%vX{`^p.RL\"><value name=\"x\"><shadow type=\"math_number_minmax\" id=\"/42-d{O6K:I=d?:4Ni~A\"><mutation min=\"0\" max=\"4\" label=\"X\" precision=\"1\"></mutation><field name=\"SLIDER\">0</field></shadow><block type=\"device_random\" id=\"K/b]u/)9bBcme3Um^@z[\"><value name=\"min\"><shadow type=\"math_number\" id=\";j;$Y68@cUhLsbznsDX6\"><field name=\"NUM\">0</field></shadow></value><value name=\"limit\"><shadow type=\"math_number\" id=\"r46{T3~Fc*b(Nt#]]Jf3\"><field name=\"NUM\">4</field></shadow></value></block></value><value name=\"y\"><shadow type=\"math_number_minmax\" id=\".*_H.3TO4T~%|ZE:]`TU\"><mutation min=\"0\" max=\"4\" label=\"Y\" precision=\"1\"></mutation><field name=\"SLIDER\">0</field></shadow><block type=\"device_random\" id=\"qy7Q9OJ@K|3:Q)ttzP9V\"><value name=\"min\"><shadow type=\"math_number\" id=\"z2{LO*LLX?S0)G:k1r%D\"><field name=\"NUM\">0</field></shadow></value><value name=\"limit\"><shadow type=\"math_number\" id=\"B@]*!46+Fh1e.Ybt@N]4\"><field name=\"NUM\">4</field></shadow></value></block></value></block></statement></block></xml>","projects/main.ts":"basic.forever(function () {\n    led.toggle(randint(0, 4), randint(0, 4))\n})\n","projects/micro-led.md":"# Micro LED\n\n## Toggle an LED\n\nDrag the code to toggle an LED\n```blocks\nbasic.forever(function () {\n    led.toggle(0,0)\n})\n```\n## Randomize x\n\nDrag the code to pick a random \"x\" index\nbetween ``0`` and ``4``\n```blocks\nbasic.forever(function () {\n    led.toggle(randint(0, 4), 0))\n})\n```\n\n## Randomize y\n\nDrag the code to pick a random \"y\" index\nbetween ``0`` and ``4``\n\n\n##All the code \n```blocks\nbasic.forever(function () {\n    led.toggle(0, randint(0, 4))\n})\n```","projects/pxt.json":"{\n    \"name\": \"unb-tutorials-engg1102-projects\",\n    \"dependencies\": {\n        \"core\": \"*\",\n        \"radio\": \"*\",\n        \"microphone\": \"*\",\n        \"unb-tutorials\": \"github:unb-ece/unb-tutorials\"\n    },\n    \"files\": [\n        \"getting-started.md\",\n        \"main.blocks\",\n        \"main.ts\",\n        \"README.md\",\n        \"micro-led.md\"\n    ],\n    \"testFiles\": [\n        \"test.ts\"\n    ],\n    \"targetVersions\": {\n        \"target\": \"9.1.1\",\n        \"targetId\": \"microbit\"\n    },\n    \"supportedTargets\": [\n        \"microbit\"\n    ],\n    \"preferredEditor\": \"tsprj\"\n}\n","projects/test.ts":"// tests go here; this will not be compiled when this package is used as an extension.\n","pxt.json":"{\n    \"name\": \"unb-tutorials-fork-engg1102\",\n    \"dependencies\": {\n        \"core\": \"*\",\n        \"unb-tutorials-fork\": \"github:joseph-xu-unb/unb-tutorials-fork\"\n    },\n    \"files\": [\n        \"README.md\",\n        \"catalog.md\",\n        \"projects/README.md\",\n        \"projects/getting-started.md\",\n        \"projects/main.blocks\",\n        \"projects/main.ts\",\n        \"projects/micro-led.md\",\n        \"p